{
  "id": "verb-position-2",
  "name": "ser and estar: the basics",
  "level": "A1",
  "variety": "es-ES",
  "es419": { "note": "ser and estar work the same in Latin America. Only the vosotros forms (sois, estáis) are replaced by ustedes son, están." },
  "subtitle": "Spanish has two verbs for “to be”. Use ser for who or what something is. Use estar for where something is and how it is right now: Soy Ana. Estoy en casa. Estoy cansada.",
  "briefMarks": ["ser", "estar"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-04)",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "ser: who? what? from where?", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "estar: where? how right now?", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">Soy</span> enfermera. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Es</span> de Perú. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Son</span> las tres. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Estoy</span> en el trabajo. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Está</span> enfermo.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>ser</strong> says <strong>who or what</strong>: name, job, nationality, origin: <em>Soy Pablo. Es médica. Son de Rumanía.</em>",
        "tag": "ser: who, what"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>ser</strong> also describes what something is <strong>like</strong>: <em>La casa es grande. Mi vecina es simpática.</em> Use it for the time and the date too: <em>Son las tres. Hoy es lunes.</em>",
        "tag": "ser: what it is like"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>estar</strong> says <strong>where</strong> a person or thing is: <em>Estoy en casa. El banco está en la plaza. ¿Dónde están las llaves?</em>",
        "tag": "estar: where"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>estar</strong> says <strong>how</strong> someone or something is right now: <em>Estoy cansado. Está enfermo. La sopa está fría.</em> Greetings use it too: <em>¿Cómo estás? – Estoy bien.</em>",
        "tag": "estar: how now"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some adjectives work with both, with a different idea: <em>Mi hijo es alto</em> (what he is like) and <em>¡Qué alto está!</em> (he has grown). At A1, learn the pairs above. More at B1.",
        "tag": "Later"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Soy cansado</em> → ✓ <em>Estoy cansado</em>. ✗ <em>Soy en casa</em> → ✓ <em>Estoy en casa</em>. ✗ <em>Estoy médico</em> → ✓ <em>Soy médico</em>. ✗ <em>Está las tres</em> → ✓ <em>Son las tres</em>. All the forms: <a href=\"/present-irregular\">Key irregular verbs</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
        {
          "caption": "The forms",
          "head": ["", "ser", "estar"],
